---
import LearnArticle from "../../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from "../../../components/FAQ.astro";

const intro =
  "An annuity turns one jackpot into decades of payments — but how is each payment actually sized? The answer is graduated-payment math: early payments are smaller, later ones larger, and the two big games do it differently. Here's the intuition, no heavy formulas required.";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: "How are lottery annuity payments calculated?",
    answer:
      "The payments form a graduated (growing) series: each payment is larger than the last by a growth rate, and the first payment is sized so that all payments add up to the advertised jackpot. With 0% growth, every payment is simply the jackpot divided by the number of payments.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why is the first annuity payment less than the jackpot divided by 30?",
    answer:
      "Because the payments grow. If every payment were jackpot ÷ 30, the series couldn't grow and still total the jackpot. With a positive growth rate, money shifts toward later years — so the first payment is smaller than an equal split, and the last payment is larger.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is Mega Millions' annuity growth rate?",
    answer:
      "5%, published in the official rules: one immediate payment, then 29 annual payments, each 5% larger than the previous one.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is Powerball's annuity growth rate?",
    answer:
      "The official rules describe 30 graduated payments over 29 years but publish no fixed rate — so any growth percentage is an assumption, not a fact. Our Powerball annuity calculator asks you to enter it (try 0% for equal payments) and labels it as your assumption.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are annuity payments taxed when calculated or when received?",
    answer:
      "When received. Each annual payment is ordinary income in the year it arrives, taxed with that year's brackets — which is why spreading payments across decades changes the tax picture versus a lump sum.",
  },
];

const sourceIds = [
  "powerball-official-rules",
  "megamillions-official-rules",
  "irs-gambling-withholding",
];
---

<LearnArticle
  slug="/learn/how-lottery-annuity-payments-work/"
  intro={intro}
  faqs={faqs}
  sourceIds={sourceIds}
>
  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">The core idea: a growing series</h2>
  <p>
    An annuity jackpot isn't divided — it's <em>scheduled</em>. The lottery
    commits to a total (the advertised jackpot) and a payment count, then
    sizes each payment so the whole series adds up to that total. With
    graduated payments, each year's check is bigger than the last by a fixed
    growth rate. Think of it as a staircase climbing toward the jackpot
    total: the first step is low, the last step is high, and the staircase as
    a whole reaches exactly the advertised number.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Why the first payment is the smallest</h2>
  <p>
    This confuses everyone, so let's be explicit. If payments grow each year
    and must still total the jackpot, the early ones <em>must</em> start below
    an equal split — otherwise the growing later payments would overshoot the
    total. With 0% growth there's no staircase at all: every payment equals
    the jackpot divided by the number of payments. The bigger the growth
    rate, the smaller the first payment and the larger the last. Nothing is
    being taken from you; it's arithmetic, not a fee.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Mega Millions: the 5% rule is published</h2>
  <p>
    Mega Millions is the transparent one. The official rules state the
    schedule exactly:{" "}
    <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">one immediate payment, then
    29 annual payments, each 5% larger than the previous one</strong>. Because
    the rate is published, the full schedule for any jackpot is determined —
    no assumptions needed. Note the first payment arrives immediately at
    claim time, unlike Powerball's structure.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Powerball: the rate is your assumption</h2>
  <p>
    Powerball's official rules describe{" "}
    <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">30 graduated payments over
    29 years</strong> — but publish no fixed graduation rate. That means any
    Powerball payment schedule you've ever seen online either asked for a
    growth assumption or silently invented one. Our{" "}
    <a href="/powerball-annuity-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      Powerball annuity calculator
    </a>{" "}
    does the honest thing: you enter the jackpot and your own annual increase
    (0% for equal payments), and it's labeled as your assumption throughout.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">What the schedule doesn't show: taxes</h2>
  <p>
    Payment schedules show <em>gross</em> amounts. In reality, each payment
    arrives net of 24% federal withholding (on amounts over $5,000), and each
    is taxed as ordinary income in the year received — which is precisely why
    the annuity's tax story differs from the lump sum's. To see every payment
    with estimated taxes applied year by year, use the{" "}
    <a href="/lottery-annuity-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      annuity calculator
    </a>{" "}
    or the game-specific{" "}
    <a href="/powerball-payout-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      payout comparison
    </a>
    .
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Try the math yourself</h2>
  <p>
    The best way to feel the staircase: build one. Enter any jackpot in the{" "}
    <a href="/powerball-annuity-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      Powerball annuity calculator
    </a>{" "}
    at 0% growth and watch every payment equal jackpot ÷ 30 — then raise the
    growth rate and watch the first payment shrink while the last one climbs.
    Same jackpot, same total, completely different shape. That's the whole
    idea.
  </p>
</LearnArticle>
